.TH JENT_STATUS 3 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "Library Functions Manual"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
.\" Too long to be justified.
.ad l
jent_status, jent_entropy_collector_uuid, jent_entropy_collector_osr, jent_entropy_collector_flags, jent_entropy_collector_memsize, jent_entropy_collector_health_failure, jent_entropy_collector_reinitializations, jent_entropy_collector_read_invocations, jent_entropy_collector_bytes_output, jent_entropy_collector_hashloops, jent_secure_memory_supported \- status of a CPU Jitter RNG entropy collector
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG library
.RI ( libjitterentropy ", " \-ljitterentropy ).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy.h>
.sp
.BI "int jent_status(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector ",
.BI "                char *" buf ", size_t " buflen );
.sp
.BI "int jent_entropy_collector_uuid(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector ",
.BI "                                char *" buf ", size_t " buflen );
.sp
.BI "#define JENT_UUID_STRLEN 37"
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_entropy_collector_osr(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_entropy_collector_flags(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "size_t jent_entropy_collector_memsize(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_entropy_collector_health_failure(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_entropy_collector_reinitializations(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "uint64_t jent_entropy_collector_read_invocations(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "uint64_t jent_entropy_collector_bytes_output(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "unsigned int jent_entropy_collector_hashloops(const struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.sp
.BI "int jent_secure_memory_supported(" void ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR jent_status ()
writes a JSON document describing an entropy collector into
.IR buf :
the library version, the identifier of the instance, its reallocations, what
it has delivered over its lifetime, the failures of each health test, the
runtime environment it found and the configuration it is running with. It is
what the
.B FIPS 140-3 CONSIDERATIONS
and
.B AIS 20/31 NTG.1 CONSIDERATIONS
sections of
.BR jitterentropy (3)
ask to be read for the properties they require. The document
is a serialization of state the instance holds, not a measurement: it costs
no entropy and collects nothing.
.LP
At most
.IR buflen
bytes are written, the terminating NUL included, and the result is always NUL
terminated. The call returns
.IR 0
when the whole document fit and
.IR \-1
when it did not - a document cut short is not handed back as if it were
complete, because a consumer would fail to parse it - as well as for a
.IR buf
of
.IR NULL
or a
.IR buflen
of zero. A buffer of 4096 bytes holds the document; that is the size the
kernel module's interfaces use for it.
.LP
The
.IR entropy_collector
may be
.IR NULL .
The result is then a complete JSON object carrying the library version alone,
which is the way to ask for the version without allocating an instance; it is
not an error and returns
.IR 0 .
.SS Status document
The document is one JSON object with these members:
.TP
.I version
The library version as a string,
.IR major.minor.patchlevel .
.TP
.I uuid
The identifier of the instance, as
.BR jent_entropy_collector_uuid ()
returns it.
.TP
.I reinitializations
As
.BR jent_entropy_collector_reinitializations ().
.TP
.I output
An object with the successful reads,
.IR invocations ,
and the
.I bytes
and
.I bits
they delivered.
.TP
.I healthFailure
An object with one member per health test:
.I apt
(adaptive proportion test),
.I rct
(repetition count test),
.I rctMemory
(repetition count test with memory) and
.I lag
(lag predictor test). Each is an object with the booleans
.I intermittent
and
.IR permanent ,
the
.B JENT_*_FAILURE
bits of
.BR jent_entropy_collector_health_failure ().
.TP
.I runtimeEnvironment
An object with
.IR cpuCores ,
the number of CPUs the library found, and
.IR cpuCache ,
an object with
.I l1Bytes
and
.IR allBytes ,
the next power of two above the size of the L1 data cache and above the sum
of all data caches, 0 where it is not known.
.TP
.I configuration
An object with the settings the instance runs with:
.RS
.TP
.I osr
The oversampling rate.
.TP
.I memoryBlockSizeBytes
The size of the memory access region in bytes, as
.BR jent_entropy_collector_memsize ()
reports it: 0 with
.BR JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS ,
where no region is allocated.
.TP
.I hashLoopCount
An object with
.IR runtime ,
the hash loop count per measurement, and
.IR initialization ,
the count the startup of the NTG.1 and FIPS modes uses while it samples the
hash loop on its own, three times the former.
.TP
.I memoryLoopCount
An object with
.I runtime
and
.IR initialization ,
the same for the memory access loop; both are 0 without memory access.
.TP
.I secureMemory
Whether the state of the collector is in secure memory, memory zeroized when
it is freed: always
.IR true ,
see
.B Secure memory
below.
.TP
.I secureMemoryBackend
The backend the state of the collector is allocated from, see
.B Secure memory
below.
.TP
.I internalTimer
Whether the instance runs on the internal timer.
.TP
.I mockedTimerBuild
Whether the library was built with its time source replaceable for testing,
the CMake option
.BR MOCK_TIMER ;
such a build produces no entropy of its own. Only in such a build, the
boolean
.I mockedTimerActive
follows it and tells whether a replacement is in use.
.TP
.I fipsMode
Whether the instance runs in FIPS mode, by
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS ,
.B JENT_NTG1
or the FIPS mode of the system.
.TP
.I ntg1Mode
Whether the instance runs in NTG.1 mode.
.TP
.I flags
An object with one boolean per flag of the instance, named after it:
.BR JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS ,
.BR JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER ,
.BR JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER ,
.BR JENT_FORCE_FIPS ,
.BR JENT_NTG1 ,
.B JENT_CACHE_ALL
and
.BR JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM ,
as
.BR jent_entropy_collector_flags ()
reports them.
.RE
.SS Instance identifier and accessors
.BR jent_entropy_collector_uuid ()
copies the identifier of an instance - an RFC 9562 version 4 or 8 UUID in its
canonical string form - into
.IR buf ,
which must hold
.B JENT_UUID_STRLEN
bytes, the terminating NUL included. The identifier is assigned once, when
the allocation has completed its startup, and is stable for the lifetime of
the instance: it is carried over when
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
reallocates the collector on a health test failure, so it names the instance
rather than the allocation. It is the
.IR uuid
member of the status document above.
.LP
The call returns
.IR 0
on success and
.IR \-1
on error, which includes a NULL
.IR entropy_collector ,
a NULL
.I buf
and a
.IR buf
too small for the string. Where the platform offers no cryptographic random
number generator to draw an identifier from, or its generator is not seeded
yet or fails, it is a version 8 UUID hashed with SHA3-256 from a process-wide
counter and the current time.
.LP
The other
.BR jent_entropy_collector_* ()
accessors read single values of an instance, the numbers the status document
reports, without its JSON:
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_osr ()
the oversampling rate, at least
.B JENT_MIN_OSR
(3), raised by each reallocation after a health test failure;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_flags ()
the
.B JENT_*
flags in effect: those of the allocation, with the memory size and hash loop
fields set to the values in effect - the memory size field as it was given
with
.BR JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS ,
which leaves it alone -,
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
added where a compliance mode or the system's FIPS mode implies it,
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
added under
.BR JENT_NTG1 ,
and, after a reallocation in FIPS mode, the timer flag of the clock in use;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_memsize ()
the size of the memory access region in bytes, 0 without memory access;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_health_failure ()
the
.B JENT_*_FAILURE
bits of the health tests standing, 0 if none. The health tests run and set
these bits in every mode, but only in FIPS mode, which
.B JENT_NTG1
implies, does a failure stop the output: without it, the bits, like the
.I healthFailure
member of the status document, are informational and reads succeed;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_reinitializations ()
the reallocations after an intermittent health test failure, each replacement
counted: those of
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe (),
and those of the startup every allocation and every such reallocation runs,
so that a collector can report one before its first read;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_read_invocations ()
the successful reads;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_bytes_output ()
the bytes those reads delivered;
.TP
.BR jent_entropy_collector_hashloops ()
the hash loop count per measurement.
.LP
The values follow the instance through its reallocations after a health test
failure: each raises the oversampling rate, doubles a memory size derived
from the caches - one the caller set stays - and doubles the hash loop count,
whether the caller set it or not, up to 128; a compile-time default above 128
stays as it is. Like the UUID, the read and byte counters are carried over
into the replacement.
.SS Secure memory
.BR jent_secure_memory_supported ()
reports whether this build provides secure memory - memory zeroized when it is
freed. Every build does, so it returns
.IR 1 ,
as the
.IR secureMemory
member of the status document reports
.IR true .
Neither reports the extras - a memory lock or a core dump exclusion - which
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
makes a condition where the platform offers them; see
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3).
The
.IR secureMemoryBackend
member of the status document names the backend the collector state comes
from, and with it which extras apply:
.IR posix-mlock ,
.IR windows-virtuallock ,
.IR libgcrypt ,
.IR openssl ,
.IR aws-lc ,
.IR linux-kernel ,
.IR freebsd-kernel ,
.I baremetal
or
.IR malloc .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_status ()
returns 0 on success and \-1 when
.I buf
is NULL or too small.
.BR jent_entropy_collector_uuid ()
returns 0 on success and \-1 for a NULL
.IR entropy_collector ,
a NULL
.I buf
or one too small.
.PP
The accessors from
.BR jent_entropy_collector_osr ()
to
.BR jent_entropy_collector_hashloops ()
return the value they read. For a NULL
.IR entropy_collector
they return 0, which no instance reports as its oversampling rate, and set
.I errno
to
.BR EINVAL ;
they leave
.I errno
alone otherwise. As a value of 0 can be a valid reading, a caller that needs
to tell the two apart sets
.I errno
to 0 before the call. The Linux and FreeBSD kernel and bare-metal builds have
no
.IR errno ;
there the 0 alone reports it.
.PP
.BR jent_secure_memory_supported ()
returns 1: every build provides secure memory, zeroized on free.
.SH ERRORS
The accessors from
.BR jent_entropy_collector_osr ()
to
.BR jent_entropy_collector_hashloops ()
set
.I errno
to:
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I entropy_collector
is NULL.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3)
for the details.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_status\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_collector_uuid\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_collector_osr\fP() to \%\fBjent_entropy_collector_hashloops\fP()
T}	MT-Safe race:entropy_collector
T{
\%\fBjent_secure_memory_supported\fP()
T}	MT-Safe
.TE
.ad
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
.BR jent_read_entropy (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3)
